    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d804">

                <pb facs="1086" n="1076" sameAs="frus:frus1945v07/pg1076"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">893.61311/4–2845</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Hurley</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to
                        the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 346</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Chungking</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1945-04-28">April 28,
                        1945</date>.<lb/>[Received May 12.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>The Ambassador has the honor to enclose, for the Department’s information, a
                    translation of a formal note dated March 24, 1945 from Dr. T. V. Soong, Minister
                    for Foreign Affairs, stating that the Chinese Government has made complete
                    settlement of the U. S. Wheat Loan to China of September 1931<anchor n="42"
                        xml:id="d804-f1"/> and of the U. S. Cotton and Wheat Loan to China of May
                        1933.<anchor n="43" xml:id="d804-f2"/>
                </p>


                <xi:include href="d804-a1.xml" xpointer="d804-a1"/>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d804-f1" n="42" xml:id="d804-f1-content">
                <p>The 1931 agreement involved the sale to the National Government of China of
                    450,000 short tons of wheat or wheat flour by the Grain Stabilization
                    Corporation with the approval of the Federal Farm Board. Payment was effected
                    through obligations of the Chinese National Government. The terms of the
                    agreement were made public on September 26, 1931, by the Federal Farm Board in
                    its press release No. 2–96 (893.48/403).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d804-f2" n="43" xml:id="d804-f2-content">
                <p>The 1933 agreement signed on May 29 provided for a Reconstruction Finance
                    Corporation loan of $50,000,000 to finance the sale of American cotton and wheat
                    to China. The details of the agreement were given in a press release issued by
                    the RFC on June 4, 1933 (893.48/708).</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
